  <h2 id="why">Why adults can learn piano very well</h2>
  <p>One of the reasons I created Pianoforall specifically for adults is that I don’t think adults need to learn the same way as children.</p>
  <p>Adults are very good at recognising patterns. If I show you three chords and then show you how those same chords appear in lots of different songs, that makes sense. If I teach you a rhythm pattern that works with hundreds of songs, you’re learning something you can use again and again.</p>
  <p>And because you already know what music sounds like, you have years of listening experience to draw on, even if you’ve never played an instrument.</p>

  <h2 id="how">How Pianoforall works</h2>
  <p>My motto with Pianoforall is <b>“Play first, ask questions later.”</b> Most traditional methods begin with reading music and theory. Pianoforall does it the other way round.</p>
  <p>You start with some simple chord shapes and rhythms and begin making music almost immediately. Reading and theory are introduced gradually. The lessons are short and carefully structured, with each new exercise adding something to what you already know. You can repeat lessons as often as you need and learn entirely at your own pace. That’s one of the great advantages of learning online.</p>
  <p>I’ve included some simple mindfulness ideas too: relaxing your shoulders, noticing tension, taking a moment before trying something again. I think being relaxed makes learning much more enjoyable. <a href="/how-it-works">More on how the method works</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="brain">What about the brain? Are we too old to learn new things?</h2>
  <p>Our brains keep changing and learning throughout life. It may take a little longer to learn some things as we get older, but that doesn’t mean we can’t learn them.</p>
  <p>For piano, regular practice is much more important than marathon sessions. Your brain and your hands need time to become familiar with new movements and patterns. Something that feels impossible today can feel surprisingly easy a few days later. That’s one of the nicest things about learning piano.</p>

  <h2 id="progress">How quickly will I progress?</h2>
  <p>I don’t like making promises such as “You’ll be playing piano in three weeks.” Everyone is different. But with Pianoforall you <em>will</em> be playing from the beginning.</p>
  <p>You start with simple chords and rhythm patterns. As they become comfortable, each new exercise adds another skill. Gradually the chords and patterns become familiar, your hands relax, and you can do more without thinking about every movement. That’s how progress happens. <a href="/learn/how-long-does-it-take-to-learn-piano">Typical timelines for adult learners</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="classical">Can older beginners learn classical piano?</h2>
  <p>Absolutely. Classical music can look intimidating because there can be hundreds of notes on the page. But many classical pieces are built around chords and repeating patterns. Once you recognise those, there’s often much less to remember than you might think.</p>
  <p>That’s the idea behind my Classics By Ear courses. I use videos, keyboard diagrams, cheat sheets and written music to teach each piece in small sections:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={PRODUCTS.moonlight.url}>Beethoven, Moonlight Sonata</a>: the first movement has repeating patterns throughout, which suits this approach well.</li>
    <li><a href={PRODUCTS.satie.url}>Erik Satie, Gnossiennes</a>: beautiful pieces with relatively simple underlying chords and patterns.</li>
    <li><a href={PRODUCTS.bach.url}>Bach Preludes</a>: understanding the patterns and chords makes these much easier to remember.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="practice">How much should you practise?</h2>
  <p>You don’t need to practise for hours. Fifteen or twenty minutes regularly is much better than one long session every week.</p>
  <p>If something is difficult, don’t keep playing the whole piece from beginning to end. Take the small section that’s causing the problem and slow it right down. Once that’s comfortable, add the next little section.</p>
  <p>And pay attention to tension. If your hands, arms or shoulders tighten, stop for a moment and relax them before starting again. Give yourself time. Your hands are learning movements they’ve probably never made before. <a href="/learn/piano-practice-routine">A simple 20-minute practice routine</a>.</p>

