  <h2 id="benefits">Are there other benefits to learning piano later in life?</h2>
  <p>For me, the best reason to learn piano is simply that you want to play music.</p>
  <p>But learning an instrument also involves concentration, memory, listening and coordination. It gives you something interesting to work on, and the satisfaction of gradually getting better at something. Playing can be very relaxing too. You have to give the music your attention, so for a while you’re not thinking about everything else going on in your life.</p>
  <p>I wouldn’t make grand claims about piano preventing ageing or transforming your brain. It’s enough that learning something new, making music and enjoying yourself are all very worthwhile things to do.</p>
